WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates — Week 19 Leader Video
Unit 5, Lesson 3: Diety

1
00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:13.020
So, when you're talking to your students about Jesus, do you automatically assume that they

2
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:17.280
believe Jesus to be God?

3
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:18.480
For years I did.

4
00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:25.280
I just assumed it until I got into a conversation with them about it, and I realized that that's

5
00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:28.480
not always the case.

6
00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.160
Not that long ago, I was actually talking with a couple of young guys that came to my

7
00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.160
door.

8
00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:34.160
They were Mormons.

9
00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:41.560
Super nice, very friendly, had a great conversation with them, and naturally I went right to talking

10
00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:48.120
about Jesus, because at first we agreed on a ton of things, which I expected to be the

11
00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:49.120
case.

12
00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:54.480
But then, once we started talking about Jesus, although it seemed like we were going

13
00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:00.160
to agree, it was very quickly realized that where I thought we would disagree is exactly

14
00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.160
where I thought we would disagree.

15
00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.160
And I went right to John 8.

16
00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:11.760
And what I love about this passage is that Jesus just sort of flat out says it.

17
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:17.080
He just says who he is, which is partially why, or almost entirely why they want the

18
00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:20.640
Pharisees and their religious leaders want to kill him, because Jesus says, before

19
00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.640
Abraham was born, I am.

20
00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:27.400
And I asked these guys, what do you think Jesus means here?

21
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:28.400
What do you think this is?

22
00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:33.840
And they said, well, they stumbled from it, and they really couldn't come up with a good

23
00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.000
answer, and they flipped to another passage and started talking about something different.

24
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.920
And I tried to redirect them back, and it reminded me of a lot of the conversations

25
00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.280
that I've had with students over the years.

26
00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:51.960
And I'm making that mistake of just assuming that when we talk about Jesus, we're talking

27
00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:53.840
about the same thing.

28
00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:58.280
So it's very possible that even in your context, when you talk about Jesus,

29
00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:01.880
you may be talking in a different plane than some of your students are.

30
00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:08.360
But this is a great opportunity to be crystal clear on exactly who it is that Jesus actually

31
00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.960
is, and who does he say he is, right?

32
00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:15.800
And that's why we have students focus right in on John 8, because it's here that Jesus

33
00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:20.080
makes the flat out statement, before Abraham was born, I am.

34
00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.880
The focus here is who Jesus claims to be.

35
00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:29.720
So we ask students in the intro activity to put down who they think they are, what

36
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:32.280
their family and friends would say about them, and then does that match up.

37
00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:33.960
And do they have evidence for this?

38
00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:36.760
Like, they think they're somebody, they think they're a soccer player, or a

39
00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:41.640
Christian, or a musician, or a football player, or whatever.

40
00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:43.760
Do they have evidence for it?

41
00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.280
I used to call myself a football player because I played football, but a person

42
00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:50.200
once brought it up to me and said, you don't really play football.

43
00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:51.040
I said, well, what do you mean?

44
00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:52.600
So we used to hit the bench.

45
00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.120
So was I really a football player?

46
00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.520
Probably not so much, right?

47
00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:00.560
So who do they think they are and what evidence for it?

48
00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:05.960
So the trick of it is, is that if Jesus says who he is, but we have

49
00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:10.600
evidence for who he says he is, now we've got something, right?

50
00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:15.560
If someone says they're a basketball player and they scored 35 points

51
00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:18.560
the night before, they're clearly a basketball player.

52
00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:20.360
There's evidence for it.

53
00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:23.240
So this is the time you can actually have your students do some of this

54
00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:28.400
interaction nicely and gently and with respect, of course, with each other

55
00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:32.360
in your large group time to kind of get this idea of, do I have evidence

56
00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:34.160
for who I think I am?

57
00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:37.560
And do I have evidence for who somebody else thinks that they are?

58
00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:40.800
So it'd be a good opportunity to do some of that interaction.

59
00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:44.840
So in your small group time, one thing that I bet is probably going to

60
00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:47.400
come out and you'll want to make sure your small group leaders are ready

61
00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:54.480
to deal with it is it's not hard, I don't think, to look at Jesus

62
00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:59.120
as a great moral teacher, as someone who has this wonderful,

63
00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:03.040
you know, religious sage kind of vibe going on where they, you know,

64
00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:07.200
they can read what he says about love and forgiveness and acceptance

65
00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.840
and mercy and justice and all these great things that Jesus talks

66
00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:15.880
about like in the Sermon on the Mount kind of stuff and leave it there.

67
00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:20.440
And then go on and say, I'm going to try to follow what Jesus says

68
00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:26.320
as a good moral teacher, but I'm not ready to accept him as the God

69
00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:31.120
of the universe, the creator of all, because if I accept him as the God

70
00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:35.440
of the universe and the creator of all, that has wider implications

71
00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:37.880
for me that I'm ready to accept.

72
00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:41.760
See, it's easy to accept him as a good moral teacher because I can

73
00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:45.360
choose to ignore his teachings if I so choose to.

74
00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:47.960
I can do whatever I want when I want.

75
00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:50.880
And when I choose to follow his teachings, I look awesome.

76
00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:54.280
If I don't, no one's really going to notice, right?

77
00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:57.240
But if I'm held accountable to the God of the universe,

78
00:04:57.240 --> 00:04:59.720
that's a whole other story.

79
00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:04.360
So believe it or not, one thing that I found amongst some of my students

80
00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:08.320
when I was back when I was teaching was that a lot of students

81
00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:14.760
really loved Jesus and really liked what he taught,

82
00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:20.480
but had very little to no intention of ever following him as God.

83
00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:22.480
And that bothered me.

84
00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:23.880
And it was right there.

85
00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:24.720
It was under my nose.

86
00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:28.120
And half the time, I don't even know if I even noticed it.

87
00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:30.000
But after a while, it became very apparent when

88
00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.800
I started looking for it.

89
00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.280
So it changed the way I taught and it changed the way

90
00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:36.000
I started to converse with some of my students.

91
00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:37.320
And he started to challenge them.

92
00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:40.320
And I think a small group in this time, in this lesson,

93
00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:42.120
might be a perfect opportunity for you

94
00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:46.240
to challenge your students in this question of how

95
00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:47.960
do you see Jesus?

96
00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:52.880
Similar to how Jesus addressed Peter in Matthew chapter 16.

97
00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:55.120
Who do you say I am?

98
00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:59.120
Ask your students, who do you say Jesus is?

99
00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:02.320
And when they answer you, whatever their answer is,

100
00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:04.320
ask them why.

101
00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:07.840
Get some answers out of them as to why they think that.

102
00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:10.200
And if they're in that place where Jesus is just

103
00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:13.920
a good moral teacher, ask them what it would take to get them

104
00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:15.680
to see him as God.

105
00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:17.320
Is there enough evidence?

106
00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:18.680
I mean, you've presented the evidence.

107
00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:19.600
There's a whole bunch of it.

108
00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:20.920
If you look through all the verses,

109
00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:23.280
we've only given you a spattering of the verses

110
00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.840
that show that Jesus is God.

111
00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:29.160
So what does that mean for them?

112
00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:31.920
Is there not enough evidence or they're just not willing?

113
00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:33.360
It can be an eye-opening experience.

114
00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:34.200
I'll be honest.

115
00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:35.680
I'm not telling you there's anything you can actually do

116
00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:38.440
about it physically right at this moment.

117
00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:41.480
But it definitely will change the way you approach those

118
00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:43.200
students, the way you address them,

119
00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:46.080
the way you teach them, the way you pray for them.

120
00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:47.680
So I would encourage you to dive into that.

121
00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:49.600
It's sort of a scary place to dive into a little bit,

122
00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:51.600
but I would encourage you to do that.

